Hi Alex / Amit / Lena (cc),

Quick technical notes and a minimal example to unblock Node integration. High-level: you will want to use the Redwood Node SDK for streaming response support and to get automatic reconnect/resume behavior for long responses. The REST streaming fallback is via chunked SSE on /v1/responses/stream.

Node SDK (pseudo-example)
const { RedwoodClient } = require('redwood-sdk');
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });

const stream = client.responses.stream({
  model: 'redwood-1.8-rl',
  input: { prompt: 'Generate product description for: SKU-4412', max_tokens: 512 },
  tool_calls: [{ name: 'price_lookup', params: { sku: 'SKU-4412' } }]
});

stream.on('data', chunk => {
  // chunk has partial tokens / tool call triggers / end markers
  process.stdout.write(chunk.text);
});

stream.on('tool_call', call => {
  // perform secure lookup against Optima sandbox API and send result back to the stream
});

If you cannot use the SDK yet, the REST streaming endpoint is:
POST https://api.redwood.com/v1/responses/stream
Headers: Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>
Body: { model: 'redwood-1.8-rl', input: { prompt: '...' } }

Tool calling flow (sandbox)
1) Model triggers a tool_call event with name and params
2) Your service calls the sandbox price API and returns a small JSON payload to the Redwood tool callback URL
3) Redwood injects the tool result into the continuing generation stream

Security notes:
- Trial logs: we keep request/response logs for 30 days in the trial by default; we can shorten retention on request or enable redaction for PII fields if you provide a mapping.
- All trial infra is isolated per-account. We can provide SOC2 artifacts upon NDA for your InfoSec if you need deeper evidence.

I’ve attached a small Postman collection and the Node snippet in redwood_sdk_snippets.zip to this thread.

